issue_context.body:
Describe the bug
When we input Ctrl+W as the first character in a new command line, we may use -1 as the index to access cmd_buf[]
in [main/subsys/shell/shell_ops.c line 164](https://github.com/zephyrproject-rtos/zephyr/blob/main/subsys/shell/shell_ops.c#L164C2-L164C60)

void z_shell_op_word_remove(const struct shell *sh)
{
	char *str = &sh->ctx->cmd_buff[sh->ctx->cmd_buff_pos - 1];
	char *str_start = &sh->ctx->cmd_buff[0];
	uint16_t chars_to_delete;

	/* Line must not be empty and cursor must not be at 0 to continue. */
	if ((sh->ctx->cmd_buff_len == 0) ||
	    (sh->ctx->cmd_buff_pos == 0)) {
		return;

Note:
case SHELL_VT100_ASCII_CTRL_W: /* CTRL + W */
sh->ctx->cmd_buff_pos will be 0 here, and result in str = &sh->ctx->cmd_buff[- 1];

Suggest str should be assigned after sh->ctx->cmd_buff_len is checked.

Please also mention any information which could help others to understand
the problem you're facing:

If ubsan compiler option is enabled, press Ctrl+W will result in error

What target platform are you using?
qemu_cortex_a53 . But we think the issue is for all platform.
What have you tried to diagnose or workaround this issue?
assign str after sh->ctx->cmd_buff_len is checked.

Expected behavior
Do not use -1 as the index when accessing cmd_buff in main/subsys/shell/shell_ops.c
